> docs\macos-m4-plan-review.md:5:1. **Make the soak an isolated measurement window.** The proposed order explicitly 
runs NAT and possibly extnet inside the two-hour soak, on the same loaded fixture. That makes packet loss, load, RSS, 
memory pressure, and capture continuity inseparable from two unrelated qualification workloads; it also gives an 
implementer an excuse for a bad soak interval. Replace the order with `VPCS -> multi-link short run -> NAT -> extnet 
disposition -> fresh clean multi-link soak -> four-node -> forced termination -> final record`. After NAT/extnet, stop 
their nodes, prove the cleanup inventory equals the pre-item baseline, and start item 6 with a fresh capture 
connection and a fixed node/link set. During the 7,200-second window prohibit node/link starts or stops, other 
hardware phases, supervisor/launcher/VM lifecycle commands, fixture reloads, and competing qualification harnesses. 
Background work may only parse or copy already-written host evidence without consuming the test VM.
  docs\macos-m4-plan-review.md:6:
  docs\macos-m4-plan-review.md:7:2. **Seal the soak before anything can invalidate or destroy its evidence.** The plan 
says to preserve evidence before item 5, but it has no objective seal. Require the collector to close normally, flush 
and `fsync` its pcapng and metric files, validate the final complete block, write final counts/duration/hashes, and 
atomically create a `SOAK-COMPLETE` manifest only after every soak bar passes. Copy/retain that manifest and all raw 
files on the Mac host, then independently re-hash and re-run the record validator. Item 5, forced termination, VM 
stop, reuse, or deletion must be refused until that seal verifies. A partial or merely closed capture must never 
unlock later phases.
  docs\macos-m4-plan-review.md:8:
  docs\macos-m4-plan-review.md:9:3. **Add explicit crash, hang, sleep, disconnect, and reboot handling for the 
two-hour run.** `INCOMPLETE` on an interrupt is not enough. Require the Mac-side harness to run independently of the 
SSH session, hold a recorded `caffeinate` assertion for the exact harness PID, verify AC power, record `pmset -g 
custom` and assertions, record `kern.boottime` at both endpoints, and inspect the bounded `pmset` sleep/wake log for 
the run interval. Add a watchdog for missed traffic, sampler, and collector heartbeats. On a hang or crash it must 
first preserve partial pcapng, metrics, process/network inventory, launcher/VM status, journals, and exit statuses in 
an immutable attempt directory; then kill only recorded owned PIDs, restore the clean baseline, and start a new 
attempt directory. A sleep, reboot, collector restart, heartbeat gap, or failed attempt requires a new uninterrupted 
7,200-second run from zero; attempts may not be resumed, concatenated, or have their samples copied into the passing 
run.
  docs\macos-m4-plan-review.md:18:
> docs\macos-m4-plan-review.md:19:8. **Narrow and mechanize the extnet exception.** The plan currently permits `NOT 
EXERCISABLE` when the accepted product contract lacks an extnet kind; that can hide an unimplemented product path even 
when Lima exposes a suitable interface. The immutable wording permits the exception only when this host's Lima 
networking exposes no suitable interface. Require a fixed decision table driven by raw `limactl` 
configuration/inventory, guest link details, host-side Lima network inventory, and the documented existing extnet 
contract. If a suitable Lima interface exists but the product contract, permissions, peer, or harness support is 
absent, the disposition is `FAIL/BLOCKED`, not `NOT EXERCISABLE`; do not add a product feature in M4. If no suitable 
Lima interface exists, record the exact failed host prerequisite and all probe exit statuses. If one exists, verify 
the known peer before attach, run bidirectional traffic, and prove cleanup. Remove the plan's locally invented “must 
not be the default-route interface” rule unless the existing extnet contract itself requires that; suitability must 
come from the actual contract, not a convenient exclusion.
  docs\macos-m4-plan-review.md:20:
> docs\macos-m4-plan-review.md:21:9. **Give every inherited hard requirement an executable gate and evidence 
artifact.** Merely requiring luna to “check each one in review” is unverified say-so. Add a named requirements matrix 
to the plan and result with these gates: (a) diff and existing profile tests prove `profiles.env` remains data and no 
Go re-encoding was added; (b) a profile test proves exact `(profile, product, build)` string matching and rejects 
near/non-equal values without numeric macOS comparison; (c) NDJSON tests inject events and wrong IDs before the 
matching string ID, assert `warnings`/`failed` after `ok:true`, and exercise raw-YAML `lab.saveDoc` versus 
structured-JSON `lab.load`; (d) hardware logs show `GET / < 500` and active endpoint retries, with Lima/systemd/node 
liveness never used as the acceptance timestamp; (e) negative cookie/bad-Origin probes fail and positive 
session-cookie plus same-origin probes pass separately on `/control`, every opened console, and capture; (f) console 
transcripts show initial wake, re-pokes, fragmented-frame accumulation, pagination disablement, last complete success 
summary, and a final prompt; (g) fixture validation rejects missing RAM or any IOL RAM below 1024 MB; and (h) `gofmt 
-l` is empty for every changed Go file, new imports are stdlib/repository-local with `go.mod` unchanged, and 
persistence sentinels prove every stop preserved data. Record each command, exit status, and artifact path; a missing 
gate blocks completion.
  docs\macos-m4-plan-review.md:22:
  docs\macos-m4-plan-review.md:23:10. **Strengthen “stop never deletes data” from a final-state assertion to a 
lifecycle invariant.** Create unique host and guest sentinel files plus manifests/hashes for images, labs, and 
unrelated user data before the first M4 start. Verify them after every `lab.stop`, launcher `stop`, recovery case, 
forced VM restart, RAM-headroom action affecting the M4 VM, and final stop. Verify the Lima machine still exists and 
no harness path invokes `delete` for the M4 VM. The forced-recovery requirement's current phrase “data remain present” 
is too weak and occurs too late to identify which stop deleted or rewrote data.
  docs\macos-m4-plan-review.md:24:
  docs\macos-m4-plan-review.md:25:11. **Remove the invitation to edit the reviewed plan and make scope enforcement a 
diff gate.** `docs/macos-m4-plan.md` appears in the “unconditional expected change set,” even though it is the 
already-produced plan and should not be an implementation output. Mark it read-only during implementation and remove 
it from expected changes. Add a final `git diff --name-status <recorded-base>...HEAD` artifact and fail the scope gate 
if any M5/M6/M7 file, frozen result/plan document, profile/port/sync/browser-equivalent redesign, or unexplained 
product/runtime file changed. Conditional runtime changes must have the already-required failing hardware evidence, 
focused regression, and affected hardware rerun before the verifier accepts them.
  docs\macos-m4-plan-review.md:35:- It explicitly protects `iol22`, uses real HTTP readiness, requires authenticated 
same-origin WebSockets and active IOS console wake behavior, preserves raw evidence, and keeps product changes 
conditional on reproduced hardware defects.
> docs\macos-m4-plan-review.md:36:- It honestly allows only the immutable extnet host-capability exception in 
principle, separates compile/unit evidence from hardware evidence, and correctly requires a full fresh soak after 
capture or runtime failure.
> docs\macos-m4-plan-review.md:37:- Keeping four-node pressure and forced termination after the soak is the right 
relationship; retain that ordering after removing NAT/extnet from the soak window.
